### What is round robin load balancing?

Round robin distributes requests by cycling through the instance list in order: first request to instance A, second to B, third to C, then back to A. It is simple, stateless, and fair when every request costs about the same and every instance is equally powerful.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class RoundRobinBalancer {

    private final List<String> instances;
    private final AtomicInteger index = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public RoundRobinBalancer(List<String> instances) {
        this.instances = instances;
    }

    public String next() {
        int i = index.getAndIncrement();
        return instances.get(i % instances.size());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new RoundRobinBalancer(List.of("account-svc-1", "account-svc-2", "account-svc-3"));
        for (int r = 0; r < 6; r++) {
            System.out.println("Request " + r + " → " + lb.next());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Request 0 → account-svc-1
Request 1 → account-svc-2
Request 2 → account-svc-3
Request 3 → account-svc-1
Request 4 → account-svc-2
Request 5 → account-svc-3
```

### What is least connections load balancing?

Least connections sends each new request to the instance currently handling the fewest active connections. This adapts to reality: if one instance is bogged down by long-running requests, its connection count stays high and it receives fewer new requests.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class LeastConnectionsBalancer {

    private final Map<String, AtomicInteger> connections = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public LeastConnectionsBalancer(java.util.List<String> instances) {
        instances.forEach(i -> connections.put(i, new AtomicInteger(0)));
    }

    public String acquire() {
        return connections.entrySet().stream()
                .min(Map.Entry.comparingByValue(
                    java.util.Comparator.comparingInt(AtomicInteger::get)))
                .map(e -> { e.getValue().incrementAndGet(); return e.getKey(); })
                .orElseThrow();
    }

    public void release(String instance) {
        connections.get(instance).decrementAndGet();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new LeastConnectionsBalancer(java.util.List.of("svc-1", "svc-2", "svc-3"));
        String first = lb.acquire();
        String second = lb.acquire();
        System.out.println("First request → " + first);
        System.out.println("Second request → " + second + " (different, both had 0 before)");
        // svc-1 now has 1 connection, svc-2 has 1, svc-3 has 0
        String third = lb.acquire();
        System.out.println("Third request → " + third + " (least connections: 0)");
    }
}
```

```text
First request → svc-1
Second request → svc-2 (different, both had 0 before)
Third request → svc-3 (least connections: 0)
```

### What is IP hash and sticky sessions?

IP hash computes a hash of the client's IP and maps it to an instance, so the same client consistently lands on the same one. Sticky sessions achieve the same using a cookie. You use these only when the instance holds client-specific local state such as an in-memory session.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.List;

public class IpHashBalancer {

    private final List<String> instances;

    public IpHashBalancer(List<String> instances) {
        this.instances = instances;
    }

    public String route(String clientIp) {
        int hash = Math.abs(clientIp.hashCode());
        return instances.get(hash % instances.size());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new IpHashBalancer(List.of("svc-1", "svc-2", "svc-3"));
        // Same IP always routes to the same instance
        System.out.println("Rajesh (10.0.0.1) → " + lb.route("10.0.0.1"));
        System.out.println("Rajesh (10.0.0.1) → " + lb.route("10.0.0.1"));
        System.out.println("Priya  (10.0.0.2) → " + lb.route("10.0.0.2"));
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh (10.0.0.1) → svc-2
Rajesh (10.0.0.1) → svc-2
Priya  (10.0.0.2) → svc-3
```

### What is consistent hashing?

Consistent hashing maps both instances and request keys onto a hash ring. Each key is served by the next instance clockwise on the ring. When you add or remove an instance, only the keys in that arc are remapped, not all keys.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.SortedMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class ConsistentHashRing {

    private final SortedMap<Integer, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();
    private static final int VIRTUAL_NODES = 100;

    public void addInstance(String instance) {
        for (int i = 0; i < VIRTUAL_NODES; i++) {
            ring.put((instance + "-vn" + i).hashCode(), instance);
        }
    }

    public void removeInstance(String instance) {
        for (int i = 0; i < VIRTUAL_NODES; i++) {
            ring.remove((instance + "-vn" + i).hashCode());
        }
    }

    public String route(String key) {
        int hash = key.hashCode();
        SortedMap<Integer, String> tail = ring.tailMap(hash);
        int target = tail.isEmpty() ? ring.firstKey() : tail.firstKey();
        return ring.get(target);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var ring = new ConsistentHashRing();
        ring.addInstance("cache-1");
        ring.addInstance("cache-2");
        ring.addInstance("cache-3");

        System.out.println("SAV2001 → " + ring.route("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("CUR3005 → " + ring.route("CUR3005"));

        // Adding cache-4 remaps only ~25% of keys, not all
        ring.addInstance("cache-4");
        System.out.println("After adding cache-4:");
        System.out.println("SAV2001 → " + ring.route("SAV2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001 → cache-2
CUR3005 → cache-1
After adding cache-4:
SAV2001 → cache-2
```

### How do you compare all the algorithms?

| Algorithm | Best For | State Needed | Weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Round Robin | Uniform requests, equal instances | None (index only) | Load-blind |
| Least Connections | Variable request durations | Live connection counts | Slightly more complex |
| Least Response Time | Heterogeneous instances | Per-instance latency | Latency measurement overhead |
| IP Hash / Sticky | Client-specific local state | None (hash function) | Uneven distribution, breaks on death |
| Weighted | Unequal capacity, canary | Weight config | Manual weight tuning |
| Consistent Hashing | Caches, shards, dynamic fleet | Hash ring | Complexity, needs virtual nodes |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using sticky sessions when the real fix is to externalize state. Stickiness undermines even load distribution and breaks if that instance dies.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

// WRONG: storing session in-memory and relying on sticky sessions
public class WrongStickyApproach {
    // Each instance holds user sessions in a local HashMap
    // If the instance dies, the session is lost
    // Sticky sessions mask the real problem: state should be external
}

// CORRECT: externalize state to Redis, no stickiness needed
public class CorrectStatelessApproach {
    // Sessions stored in shared Redis
    // Any instance can serve any request
    // Load balancer uses round robin or least connections freely
}
```

### Why must every algorithm include health checks?

Distributing traffic evenly to a dead instance is worse than useless. Every algorithm must be combined with health checks so dead instances are removed from rotation.

```java
package com.bank.lb;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class HealthAwareBalancer {

    private final List<String> allInstances;

    public HealthAwareBalancer(List<String> instances) {
        this.allInstances = new ArrayList<>(instances);
    }

    public List<String> getHealthy() {
        // In production: periodic HTTP health check to each instance
        return allInstances.stream()
                .filter(i -> !i.equals("svc-2")) // svc-2 failed health check
                .toList();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var lb = new HealthAwareBalancer(List.of("svc-1", "svc-2", "svc-3"));
        System.out.println("Healthy instances: " + lb.getHealthy());
        System.out.println("svc-2 removed from rotation after failed health check");
    }
}
```

```text
Healthy instances: [svc-1, svc-3]
svc-2 removed from rotation after failed health check
```

